# ComposeUX Board Bring-Up [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

> Bringing up a board is currently an ad-hoc flash loop rediscovered per device. Make it a descriptor: one machine-readable per-board file says how to connect, flash, and verify; the markdown recipe becomes a generated human view of it; a control surface consumes it to stand up any node and record how to rebuild it. The verdict is two-tier — machine-green is necessary but a human eyes-on-glass gate is what actually accepts a board.

---

## 1. Intent

### [CXC-BRINGUP-INTENT] Systematize Bring-Up, From Flash Loops to a Descriptor Surface

- problem: every new board is a fresh ad-hoc flash loop with the hard-won facts scattered across a platformio.ini, commit messages, and a person's memory. The real cost is concrete and recent: **this cycle's CYD shared-SPI-DMA-channel hunt** (a staged hardware bisection to find that an SD-bus `spi_bus_free()` teardown froze the panel while it kept ACKing writes) and **the CH32V203 wchisp/BOOT0 dance** (a manual ISP strap that is easy to get wrong and has no software entry) both cost real hours that a descriptor + a surface would have front-loaded.
- definition: a bring-up discipline where each board has ONE machine-readable descriptor (`docs/board-bringup/<board>.descriptor.yaml`) carrying identity, connect, flash, config-source, requires, verify, and quirks — and a control surface that consumes it to connect/flash/verify/record a node. Same discipline as [CXC-HAL]: define-once descriptor over per-platform drivers, generalized from *binding a peripheral* to *standing up a whole board*.
- grounded: FOUR real boards brought up this cycle prove the schema is not hypothetical — the constrained SPI-display floor (CYD), the framebuffer-tier round panel (Qualia S3), the reactive/actuator RISC-V tier (ESP32-C3 devkit + lego_dots + rack_triple_gauge), and the first non-ESP32/non-esptool part (CH32V203, wchisp). Four divergent ad-hoc descriptor shapes reconciled into one.
- payoff: a new board = author a descriptor + (if novel) register its flash tool, not reinvent a flash loop; a new physical unit = point the surface at it and press one gated button.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 2. The Descriptor

### [CXC-BRINGUP-DESCRIPTOR] The Per-Board Descriptor Is the Single Source; the Recipe Is a Generated View

- define-once: the descriptor is the SINGLE SOURCE. The markdown recipe (`docs/board-bringup/<board>.md`) demotes to a **generated human view**; the app/surface consumes the descriptor, not the prose — exactly the [CXC-HAL-PLATFORM] "define-once descriptor" move applied to a board. Divergence between recipe and descriptor becomes impossible because the recipe is rendered FROM the descriptor.
- one schema, all boards (the deliverable is the consistency): identical top-level keys, same order, across all four — `board_id / name / chip{part,arch,flash_kb,ram_kb} / connect{bus,kind,port_glob,baud} / flash{tool,cmd,enter_bootloader,retries,notes} / config_source / requires / verify{machine,human} / quirks / variants`. Board-specific detail hangs *under* those keys; the shape never changes, so the future generalized bring-up surface consumes any board uniformly.
- variants where one board_id fronts several units: ESP32-C3's `variants:` sub-list carries the three C3 targets (headless devkit + lego_dots 12ch + rack_triple_gauge) — shared chip/connect/flash at the top, per-variant env/role/pins/actuators/quirks below. CYD/Qualia/CH32V203 keep `variants: []` (single board) so the key still exists — consistency preserved.
- requires: = reproducible-from-the-bus: board-specific images/packages/blobs a bring-up needs are named as `requires:` references (David's directive — any image/package a board needs lives in `bus_storage` and is fetched at bring-up, reproducible from the bus). Grounded: the CYD/Qualia scene config (`bus://assets/displays/<device_id>.compose.yaml`, the [CXC-R-BOOT] fungible-fetch) and the animation assets (`bus://assets/animations/dradis/*`, CI16 sprite frames baked offline). Where an artifact does not exist yet (a bus-hosted factory firmware image, a board package-set), it is recorded as a `null` placeholder WITH a comment naming where it WOULD live (`bus://assets/firmware/<board>/<ver>.bin`) — the gap is explicit, not silent. Non-bus prerequisites are honestly typed (the C3's gitignored `src/wifi_config.h` secret, the `mqtt://mqtt-broker.local:1883` transport) rather than faked as bus assets.
- quirks are traceable, each: every hard-won workaround is a `{id, value?, resolved?, note}` entry citing its recipe section/commit — the CYD DMA hazard (resolved, 9a35baf+cfa313a), the 27->20MHz SPI margin (resolved), the Qualia pclk 16->10MHz + 20-line bounce + IRAM_SAFE=n + RESTART_IN_VSYNC panel tune, the C3 strapping-pin/BC337 boot risk + rack out_max=600 placeholder, and the CH32V203 >64KB per-unit flash + the canon_equality char-signedness finding. A quirk is never lore; it is a descriptor row.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 3. The Verdict

### [CXC-BRINGUP-VERDICT] Two-Tier Acceptance — Machine-Green != Glass-Green

- the key design point: bring-up is accepted on TWO tiers, not one. `verify.machine` is the set of auto-checkable signals (serial pass-tokens like "234/234" or "RGB panel up: 720x720", a `wchisp` clean verify, a capture-pixel-diff vs the Skia reference, a host-unit "ALL PASS (94 checks)"). `verify.human` is the eyes-on gate WITH its reason — and it is load-bearing, not ceremony.
- motivating evidence (why the human tier exists at all): the CYD's real failure this cycle was **the framebuffer/render loop advancing normally while SPI writes kept returning `ESP_OK` and the glass stayed frozen on frame 0**. A healthy `[PERF]` serial stream AND a moving `capture.bmp` were BOTH insufficient — the machine tier was fully green while the panel was dead. Only eyes-on-glass caught it. Machine-green != glass-green is therefore a design invariant, sourced from a real silent-ACK failure.
- the human gate is board-specific and reasoned: CYD = confirm live motion on the glass (silent SPI-ACK freeze); Qualia = eyes on the round glass via the pre-network color-wheel, because the ~1.5MB `/capture.bmp` truncates (IncompleteRead) and cannot be the gate; ESP32-C3 actuators = 12V + eyes on real light/needle/backlight motion (not automatable from the HAL self-report); CH32V203 = confirm the manual ISP strap enumerated (0x55e0) and per-unit-verify any >64KB image. Each `human:` string carries its WHY so the surface can present it, not just assert it.
- machine tier is necessary, not sufficient: it gates *fast* (catches the 3/234 canon_equality regressions, a dirty wchisp verify, a pixel-diff structural divergence) and is what a headless CI/dispatcher can run — but it never closes bring-up on a board with a physical output. The two tiers compose: machine-green THEN human-green.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 4. Dispatch

### [CXC-BRINGUP-DISPATCH] Flash-Tool + Entry Dispatch Off the Descriptor

- `flash.tool` is the dispatch key: the surface branches on it — `esptool` (the three ESP families, via PlatformIO) vs `wchisp` (the WCH part, a Rust CLI). A new toolchain = a new dispatch branch + a descriptor that names it, not a rewrite of the bring-up flow.
- `enter_bootloader` is the second axis, and it is genuinely bimodal: `auto` (esptool: the CH340 auto-reset on the CYD, or native-USB auto-download on the S3/C3 — no button dance) vs `boot0-reset-manual` (wchisp: the CH32V203's physical hold-BOOT0 / tap-reset / release strap, with NO DTR/RTS software trick — `automatable:false`). The surface must PROMPT for the manual strap and confirm ISP enumeration before dispatching `wchisp flash`; it can fire the esptool path unattended.
- `retries:` is declarative recovery: the native-USB `errno6-native-usb` transient re-enumeration (Qualia + C3) is a named, expected "retry the upload once" — the surface handles it instead of a human re-guessing. The CH340 CYD path and the atomic `wchisp flash` (write+verify+reset in one) carry empty `retries:`.
- notes carry the tool caveats the dispatcher must respect: `wchisp` install (`cargo install wchisp --locked` needing libusb + a set rustup default), the stale-`pio pkg install` Windows-toolchain lock, the `>64KB` `maximum_size=131072` override, the `-Os`-only soft-double constraint. Dispatch is descriptor-driven end to end.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 5. The Surface

### [CXC-BRINGUP-SURFACE] Generalize [minnow-bringup] — One Control Surface Stands Up Any Node

- the seed exists: `apps/minnow-bringup/` is already a ComposeUX control surface over node provisioning — a `.compose.yaml` form (hostname, a locally-hashed `$6$` password, option checkboxes) whose Provision button is an **mcp_action** that resolves the form namespaces into `fleetconsole.provision_run` params. This spec generalizes it from "provision a MinnowBoard" to "bring up ANY node from its descriptor."
- allowlisted + gated mcp_actions (the safety model, already realized): the surface exposes a fixed **allowlist** (`_ACTIONS`, [MCPA-5]) — an un-listed tool is REFUSED loudly; destructive actions are flagged. Live effect is **gated behind an explicit env** (`FLEET_BRINGUP_LIVE=1` + the target powered on) so the default path validates+assembles the plan and stops short of firing the serial install — safe and headless-verifiable. The bring-up surface inherits this: flashing/provisioning a real board is a gated, allowlisted action, and the same surface reuses fleetpower's guarded HA plug primitives (single-sourced allowlist) to power the DUT.
- credentials never held: the secret field hashes locally ($6$) and only the hash reaches the driver — the agent/surface never holds plaintext; the driver refuses a non-`$6$` value loudly. A bring-up surface handling WiFi creds (the C3's `wifi_config.h`) or a device password rides the same never-hold-plaintext rule.
- auto-capture to `doc://fleet_devices` = provisioning IS recording the rebuild spec: on provision, `_capture_spec` MERGES the recipe/serial-device/params (incl. the just-used `$6$` hash) into `doc://fleet_devices/<host>` — so standing a node up is the act of recording how to rebuild it, non-clobbering, best-effort. For MCU bring-up the analogue is capturing the flashed `board_id`/`env`/image + verify results to the same fleet-device doc, closing the reproducible-from-the-bus loop with `requires:`.
- Restore: the captured spec + the `requires:` bus references are exactly what a **Restore** needs — re-fetch the images/config from `bus_storage`, re-flash via the descriptor's dispatch, re-verify. Restore is the descriptor + capture read back.
- UNIFIES the fleet story: one surface stands up any node — a Linux SBC (the MinnowBoard fleetconsole path, [CXC-RM-RUNTIME-DIST]'s Pi/SBC tier) OR an MCU (esptool/wchisp per the descriptor). The Linux-provision surface and the MCU-flash surface are one control surface over one descriptor shape; the reactive tier ([SPEC:CXC-EMBED-REACTIVE]) is what then RUNS on the MCU node the surface stood up.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 6. Open Decisions (before build)

### [CXC-BRINGUP-OPEN] What Needs Deciding

- **descriptor bus home**: the descriptors are LOCAL-FIRST today (`docs/board-bringup/*.descriptor.yaml`, eyeballed before pushing). Their ultimate home is `doc://board_profiles/*` on the bus (sibling to `doc://fleet_devices` and `doc://surfaces`), fetched at bring-up — PENDING; do not push until the schema is owner-ratified.
- **recipe generation direction**: confirm the recipe becomes a rendered view OF the descriptor (single source) vs. the descriptor staying a hand-maintained sibling; leaning generated-from-descriptor.
- **the char-signedness DSL fix (CH32V203)**: 3/234 canon_equality failures are WCH-toolchain-specific (ESP32-C3, also RISC-V, passes 234/234). Try `-fsigned-char` first, else patch the canon-equality path; re-run the corpus on hardware — must not be assumed fixed. Feeds [CXC-DSL].
- **per-unit extended-flash verification**: the CH32V203 `>64KB` (`maximum_size=131072`) relies on unmarked per-unit silicon margin; the surface should REQUIRE a clean per-unit `wchisp` verify of a 64-96KB image before trusting extended flash, with CH32V203RB (128KB official) as the recorded fallback.
- **mcp_action schema for flashing**: reuse the `[minnow-bringup]` allowlist+gate shape for a generic `flash_run(board_id, variant, env)` action, or a per-tool action set — bound the set (esptool vs wchisp) and the gate env.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

---

## 7. The Bus-Native Peripheral (a device with onboard logic, not a panel)

### [CXC-BRINGUP-PERIPHERAL] A Bus-Native Peripheral Is a Distinct Bring-Up Shape from a Panel

- shape: the rest of this spec brings up a PANEL — silicon + a descriptor + a colour baseline on glass. A bus-native PERIPHERAL (a sensor or actuator with ONBOARD logic that joins ComposeBus as a peer — e.g. the R503 fingerprint sensor) is verified not by a colour on glass but by the SEMANTIC EVENTS it puts on the bus. Its decision logic runs ON the device by design.
- stance: the "host-motherboard" model (a thin device streaming raw telemetry to a Python brain on the mac, per the throttle controller) is NOT the default for such a device. That pattern earns its keep for a sensor front-end whose policy naturally lives host-side; for a device whose LOCAL decision is a designed property (a biometric match + auth decision that must not round-trip to a host), moving the brain off-device removes that property — and the debugging advantages it offered are already supplied by [CXC-BRINGUP-MOCK-HAL-FIRST] (the twin) and the per-device diagnostic widget. (R503 assessment, David + architect 2026-08-19.)
- companion: `.claude/skills/bringup-bus-native-peripheral` (the operational checklist); the panel case is `docs/board-bringup/RUNBOOK.md`.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

### [CXC-BRINGUP-MOCK-HAL-FIRST] Build the Device Logic Mock-HAL-First, Host-Testable, Twin-Backed

- method: build the full decision logic against a MOCK HAL before the silicon exists, and keep the core PURE and host-testable — the `*_canonical.cpp` idiom (one source shared by the host test and the firmware), with every ESP/vendor call behind `#ifdef ESP_PLATFORM` or an INJECTED seam (a `std::function` the host test fills with a fake and the firmware fills with the real one). The size-guard, the failure paths, the state machine are all proven on the mac before any hardware exists.
- twin: keep a REFERENCE TWIN of the device logic (Python) and assert BOTH the twin and the C++ firmware against ONE shared selftest golden. The golden enforces byte-parity so the firmware cannot silently drift from the reference; and the twin is the off-hardware executable debugger — to reproduce or step through any device behaviour, run the twin. This is strictly better than a separate host-side "motherboard" implementation, which would itself drift.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

### [CXC-BRINGUP-SEMANTIC-GLASS] The Peripheral's Glass Tier Is Two Instruments Agreeing on Semantic Events

- refines [CXC-BRINGUP-VERDICT] for a device with no panel: machine-green is `native_test` ALL PASS plus the firmware target compiling. The GLASS tier is TWO INDEPENDENT INSTRUMENTS that must agree — the human on serial (the internal narrative: receive, decode, fetch, load) AND your own bus subscriber catching the device's SEMANTIC EVENTS (heartbeat state, the domain event, the failure taxonomy on the wire). A green self-report on serial alone is not the verdict.
- live-test: seed the upstream (store/service), publish the command, watch BOTH instruments for the expected semantic events. Two disciplines make the test real: the fixture must be REPRESENTATIVE (for binary, bytes WITH embedded nulls — an all-ASCII fixture is an instrument that cannot fail, and a fake-resolver host test never exercises the real fetch); and watch the INSTRUMENT WINDOW (a listener that expired before the device responded proves nothing — relaunch it long, then re-publish; an idempotent command re-acks safely).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]
- relates-to: [CXC-BRINGUP-VERDICT]

### [CXC-BRINGUP-OPAQUE-REF] Opaque Binary Rides Behind a Reference, Fetched Binary-Safe

- rule: a peripheral's opaque binary (templates, images, audio) does NOT cross the JSON bus inline or base64'd ([DP-CIO-18]); it rides behind a REFERENCE (`<scheme>://<opaque-id>`) resolved against a DEDICATED store (its own port, never bus_storage's), with the store endpoint CONFIG not hardcoded ([DP-CIO-6]).
- fetch: the device fetches by content-length into RAW BYTES — never an Arduino `String`/`getString()`, which truncates at the first embedded null and silently corrupts any real binary (measured on R503's 1536-byte templates). A fetch failure surfaces on the SAME failure path a HAL write failure would (reuse it; do not fork a new one).
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-BRINGUP]

